// run
// #4545: the runtime half of `check_types_assign_arrow_recv.bit`. Two functions
// each declare a local named `b`, of a DIFFERENT class each, and each assigns an
// arrow literal to a field of it. `assignTargetFuncType` (checkarrow.bit) decides
// the expected type of that arrow; until #4545 it read a map keyed by the
// receiver's NAME and flat across the module, so the LAST `b` declared anywhere
// decided every `b`, `A.f` was looked up on `B` and found nothing, and `one`'s
// arrow parameter stayed `<error>`.
//
// That is #2749 again and it reaches LOWERING: `"yo " + name` emitted a raw
// `add` over two string pointers instead of `rt_call string_concat`. Measured on
// `main` at f079d131, five runs of this program: segmentation fault, `panic: out
// of memory`, then three SIGKILLs — never the same fault twice, and `bit check`
// exited 0 every time. #4531's note that a wrong answer here "is a wrong dump,
// not a wrong binary" was wrong, which is why this case is `// run` and not only
// `// types`.
class A { f: (string) => string }

class B { g: (int) => int }

fn one(): string {
  let b = A{ f: (v) => v }
  b.f = (name) => "yo " + name
  return b.f("x")
}

fn two(): int {
  let b = B{ g: (v) => v }
  b.g = (num) => num + 1
  return b.g(1)
}

fn main() {
  print(one() + "\n")
  print("${two()}\n")
}
